{"role":"assistant","content":"Fixed. The change is one word in `gen.py:13` — the HR corridor's lower bound is now `\"start\"` instead of `\"value\"`.\n\n**Why:** Intervals' FIT export switches modes on that key name. `{value, end}` is exported as a Lap-HR / zone-mapped target, so the watch resolves it against its own auto-detected zones — exactly the clamping in `readback/watch-notes.md` (watch LTHR 168's bands 118–137, 151–162, 137–151 instead of the planned 117–129, 151–160, 129–141). `{start, end}` becomes an Instant-HR corridor and the watch shows the raw bpm Intervals computed from its own stored LTHR (172), independent of the watch's zones.\n\nVerified by running `python3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json` — output now emits `\"start\": lo, \"end\": hi` for all four step targets. With the fix, the watch should render the expected corridors from the notes table.\n\n**Colleague's note** (detailed in `ANSWER.md`): I applied the core diagnosis and one-word fix, the corroboration of the failed `bpm`/`start_bpm` attempts, and the keep-percentages advice. I did not apply the top-level `description` text-grammar part — that's a web-UI visibility trap, and our symptom was watch-side clamping only, with steps already populating fine."}
